I can help you get organized and ready to talk with Sarah so you can make an informed decision about settlement vs. trial. Before I give the detailed list, one quick clarification: you said this involves divorce proceedings and also “small claims court.” Are these two separate matters (a divorce case plus a related small-claims case), or is the dispute being handled entirely in small claims? That affects which documents and procedures apply. If you’re not sure, mention that to Sarah and I’ll proceed with the general checklist below that covers both divorce-related and small-claims issues.

What to ask your attorney — grouped and ready to use in a call or email

A. Case posture, likely outcomes, and strategy
- What are the realistic possible outcomes at the upcoming hearing (best case, worst case, and most likely)? Please give a numerical or dollar range if possible.
- What is our estimated probability of getting the outcome you described (approximate %)?
- What are the strongest facts/evidence for our side? What are the weakest?
- What are the judge’s likely priorities/concerns in this type of matter in Maricopa County?
- Are there any legal or factual issues that could surprise us at hearing?

B. Settlement-specific questions
- Is the current settlement offer reasonable compared to what we’d likely get at trial after costs and risks? If so, why; if not, why not?
- What counter-offer would you recommend (amounts, payment schedule, non-monetary terms)?
- What are the pros and cons of settling now vs. going to hearing/trial (cost, time, risk, confidentiality, emotional toll)?
- If we accept, what specific terms do we need (release language, payment schedule, security, interest, attorney-fee shifting, confidentiality, non-disparagement)?
- If we reject, how likely is it we can obtain a better net result at trial (after legal fees and enforcement risks)?
- Is mediation a good next step? Will the court require or suggest mediation?

C. Costs, timing, and financial analysis
- What is your current estimate of additional attorney fees and court costs if we go to hearing/trial?
- How long, realistically, until a hearing/trial and until final resolution (including post-judgment enforcement or appeals)?
- What is our “break-even” point — the settlement amount at which settlement is financially preferable to proceeding (i.e., expected trial award × probability − expected additional costs = compare to settlement)?
- What are the potential tax consequences or collateral effects of the settlement terms?

D. Evidence, witnesses, and preparation
- What documents will the court expect or require for the hearing? (Examples: financial declarations, pay stubs, tax returns, bank statements, receipts, invoices, lease deeds/title, communications, discovery responses, deposition transcripts, expert reports, appraisal.)
- What additional documents should we try to obtain now that discovery closed?
- Which witnesses should we plan to call? Will their testimony be persuasive? Can they be subpoenaed if needed?
- Do we need any expert witnesses or valuations? How long and how much will experts take/cost?
- How should we organize and label exhibits (numbering, copies for judge/opponent/witness)?
- Are there any evidentiary problems we should anticipate (hearsay, authentication, inadmissible evidence)?

E. Procedural and tactical items
- Are there pre-hearing filings we need to submit (pretrial statement, exhibit list, witness list, motions in limine)?
- Are there time limits at the hearing? Any special courtroom procedures in Maricopa County to be aware of?
- Do we need to prepare an order or settlement agreement draft now (so a settlement can be executed quickly)?
- Can we get an interim/temporary order if needed?
- If settlement talks break down, is there a short tactical step you recommend (e.g., depositions, targeted motion) before trial?

F. Enforcement, judgment, and post-judgment options
- If we obtain a judgment or settlement, how do we enforce it (judgment lien, wage garnishment, levy, contempt)?
- What additional costs and timeline for enforcement should we expect?
- If we settle, what happens to pending claims or counterclaims (do we need mutual releases)?
- If we lose, what are our options for appeal or post-judgment relief (and the deadlines for those)?

G. Personal and practical considerations
- How much risk are we comfortable taking? (Ask Sarah’s opinion about whether you should accept a “guaranteed but smaller” amount now vs. longer odds for more later.)
- How will a trial affect non-financial issues you care about (privacy, stress, family)?
- Would a partial settlement or staged settlement make sense (resolve some issues now, keep others for hearing)?

Checklist of documents to bring to your meeting or to have ready
- Case docket and timeline (dates of filing, discovery, hearings)
- Copies of all discovery produced and received (interrogatories, requests for production, deposition transcripts)
- Financial records: pay stubs, tax returns (last 2–3 years), bank statements, credit card statements, account ledgers
- Bills/receipts/invoices that support monetary claims (itemized)
- Contracts, titles, deeds, leases, ownership documents
- Communications: text messages, emails, social media messages that are relevant
- Appraisals or valuations, repair estimates, expert reports
- Witness contact information and brief summary of what each witness can say
- Any prior settlement offers and correspondence about negotiation
- Proposed settlement language or draft agreement (if you have one)

Simple decision framework (quick calculation)
- Estimate likely trial award (A)
- Estimate probability of winning that award (P)
- Estimate additional legal and enforcement costs if we go to trial (C)
- Expected trial value = P × A − C
- Compare expected trial value to the settlement offer (S). If S > expected trial value (and you value certainty/time), settlement may be preferable. Factor in non-monetary values (stress, confidentiality).

